    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d426">

                <pb facs="0663" n="561" sameAs="frus:frus1935v01/pg561"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.90H/33: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Straus</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Paris</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1935-06-29T11:00:00+01:00">June 29, 1935—11
                            a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received June 29—9:15 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>548. Department’s 209, June 6, 5 p.m. Afghans still unwilling to accept
                    phraseology of article 4 in accordance with the Department’s original draft,
                    being embarrassed by the use of the words “most favored nation” treatment.</p>
                <p>In order to get around this difficulty might I be authorized<anchor n="10"
                        xml:id="d426-f1"/> to propose the substitution of the text of present
                    article 4 by the article on the most favored nation clause contained on page 12
                    of the general provisions for inclusion in trade agreements forwarded with
                    Department’s circular instruction diplomatic serial number 2572 of June 6,
                        1935.<anchor n="11" xml:id="d426-f2"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Straus</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d426-f1" n="10" xml:id="d426-f1-content">
                <p>Authorization was granted in Department’s telegram No. 263, July 3, 6 p.m.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d426-f2" n="11" xml:id="d426-f2-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
